---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'Career options after MA in India: the 4 real doors once the degree is done';
const description =
  'Career options after MA in India span academia via NET-JRF-PhD, civil services, teaching, and corporate roles. Real pay, exam odds, and a decision test for each door.';
const canonical = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/career-options-after-ma-in-india';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-07-16';
const publishedDateLabel = '16 July 2026';
const authorName = 'Allu Vagdevi';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/allu-vagdevi/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'question', label: 'The real question after an MA' },
  { id: 'doors', label: 'The 4 doors, compared' },
  { id: 'academia', label: 'Academia and research: NET, JRF, PhD' },
  { id: 'civil-services', label: 'Civil services: UPSC and State PSC' },
  { id: 'teaching', label: 'Teaching: school PGT/TGT vs college' },
  { id: 'corporate', label: 'Corporate, policy, and industry roles' },
  { id: 'other-govt', label: 'SSC, banking, and PSU exams beyond UPSC' },
  { id: 'cost-timeline', label: 'The real cost and timeline map' },
  { id: 'protocol', label: 'The 4-Fit Protocol: how to actually choose' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Mistakes that cost people 2-3 years' },
  { id: 'test', label: 'Test a door before you commit to it' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const doorTable = [
  {
    door: 'Academia and research (NET, JRF, PhD)',
    bestFor: 'People who want to keep working inside the subject itself and are fine with a slow, funded runway before a stable salary.',
    reality: 'JRF stipend runs roughly Rs 37,000-42,000 a month; a PhD typically takes 3-6 years; a permanent assistant professor post is not guaranteed even after NET and a PhD.',
  },
  {
    door: 'Civil services (UPSC, State PSC)',
    bestFor: 'People who want the widest generalist government career and can fund 1-3 serious attempts without needing income immediately.',
    reality: 'The overall UPSC CSE selection ratio was roughly 0.1% in the 2025 cycle. State PSCs have softer odds but far lower pay and slower recruitment cycles.',
  },
  {
    door: 'Teaching (school PGT/TGT, or college via NET)',
    bestFor: 'People who want structured hours, direct classroom work, and a path that starts paying sooner than research or civil services.',
    reality: 'School PGT posts typically pay Rs 30,000-55,000 a month in government schools; private schools often pay less. College teaching needs NET or a PhD first.',
  },
  {
    door: 'Corporate, policy, and industry roles',
    bestFor: 'People who want to start earning within months and are willing to add one visible, market-facing skill on top of the MA.',
    reality: 'Entry-level pay is often modest (roughly Rs 3-6 LPA) without a multiplier skill, but the pay ceiling and speed to first income both beat the other three doors.',
  },
];

const academiaCards = [
  {
    label: 'Entry route',
    title: 'UGC NET decides three separate outcomes at once, not just one',
    body: 'Clearing NET alone makes you eligible to teach. Clearing NET with a JRF rank funds a PhD. A separate NET-JRF-only category exists purely for research funding, without a teaching-eligibility claim. Read your scorecard carefully before assuming which door it opened.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Reality check',
    title: 'The funded years are real, but the permanent job at the end is not automatic',
    body: 'JRF pays roughly Rs 37,000 a month for the first two years, rising to about Rs 42,000 as a Senior Research Fellow. A PhD typically runs 3-6 years. Permanent assistant professor vacancies do not open every year in every subject, so plan for a wait even after you finish.',
  },
];

const civilServicesCards = [
  {
    label: 'Why an MA genuinely helps here',
    title: 'Subject overlap with General Studies and optional papers is real, not marketing',
    body: 'Political science, sociology, history, public administration, economics, and geography MAs overlap directly with UPSC General Studies content and several optional subjects, which is a genuine head start over graduates from unrelated fields.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Reality check',
    title: 'The odds are the odds, regardless of how well your MA fits the syllabus',
    body: 'The 2025 Civil Services Examination saw roughly 5.77 lakh candidates appear against 1,087 vacancies, and 958 final selections — an overall ratio near 0.1%. Subject fit improves your odds inside the exam; it does not change the exam\'s brutal base rate.',
  },
];

const teachingCards = [
  {
    label: 'Entry route',
    title: 'School teaching (PGT/TGT via state boards or CTET/state-TET) pays sooner; college teaching needs NET or a PhD first',
    body: 'Government school PGT and TGT recruitment runs through state-level exams or CTET-linked hiring, generally faster to a paycheck than the NET-to-vacancy college route.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Reality check',
    title: 'Government pay beats private pay by a wide margin, and it is the direct trade-off',
    body: 'Government school PGT pay typically runs Rs 30,000-55,000 a month with allowances; many private schools pay Rs 15,000-30,000 for the same designation. Government college assistant professor basic pay starts near Rs 57,700 (roughly Rs 90,000-1.1 lakh gross); private colleges commonly pay Rs 40,000-60,000 for the same title.',
  },
];

const corporateCards = [
  {
    label: 'What actually gets you hired',
    title: 'The MA subject rarely reads as the qualifying skill on its own; a paired, visible skill does',
    body: 'HR and L&D, content strategy, policy research, CSR, corporate communications, market research, and UX writing all hire MA graduates regularly, but almost always alongside a certification, a portfolio, or a demonstrated tool skill layered on top of the degree.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Reality check',
    title: 'Slower ceiling talk, faster starting line',
    body: 'Entry-level research associate, HR executive, or content roles commonly start around Rs 3-6 LPA. That is lower than a cleared UPSC posting or a permanent professorship, but it starts now, not after years of exam prep or a PhD.',
  },
];

const costTimelineRows = [
  {
    item: 'NET preparation and attempt (subject-specific, MA is direct eligibility)',
    range: 'Usually 6-12 months of focused prep alongside or after the MA. Exam fee is modest; the real cost is time and, for some, coaching material.',
  },
  {
    item: 'JRF-funded PhD (if NET clears the JRF cutoff)',
    range: 'Typically 3-6 years, funded at roughly Rs 37,000-42,000 a month through the JRF stipend, rising after the second year.',
  },
  {
    item: 'ICSSR Doctoral Fellowship (if JRF is not cleared but NET is)',
    range: 'A strictly two-year term, funded at roughly Rs 20,000 a month plus an annual contingency grant, open mainly to social science and humanities scholars.',
  },
  {
    item: 'UPSC or State PSC preparation, if you go the civil services route',
    range: 'Coaching and materials commonly run Rs 1.5-3 lakh per prep year in a coaching hub, on top of foregone income of roughly Rs 3-6 lakh a year, across 1-3 realistic attempts.',
  },
  {
    item: 'State-level PGT/TGT or CTET-linked teaching recruitment',
    range: 'Exam-to-posting timelines vary by state, but this is generally the fastest exam-gated route to a first stable paycheck on this list, often within a year of clearing the recruitment exam.',
  },
  {
    item: 'A corporate certification or portfolio layer (HR, content, data, research methods)',
    range: 'Usually 2-6 months and a few thousand rupees to a low lakh, depending on the certification, and often the single fastest route to a first paycheck of all six rows here.',
  },
];

const protocolSteps = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Runway: how many months can you go without stable income',
    body: 'Academia and civil services both ask for years of low or no income before any payoff. If your household cannot fund that runway, corporate or teaching (which pay sooner) are not a lesser choice — they are the honest one for your situation right now.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Risk appetite: can you handle a real chance of the door not opening at all',
    body: 'A PhD without a guaranteed post, or a UPSC attempt with a roughly 0.1% overall selection ratio, both carry a genuine chance of no payoff after years of effort. Teaching and corporate roles have a real ceiling, but a far higher chance of some income along the way.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Subject-market fit: does your specific MA subject actually feed the door you want',
    body: 'Political science, sociology, and public administration feed UPSC General Studies directly. English, psychology, and economics feed corporate and research roles more directly than they feed most State PSC syllabi. Check the actual overlap before assuming your subject fits every door equally.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Timeline to first income: pick a door your actual life stage can support',
    body: 'Someone with family financial pressure and a real cost of delay usually needs the corporate or teaching door first, even if academia or civil services stays a longer-term goal running in parallel through NET, JRF, or a part-time exam attempt.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Treating NET, JRF, UPSC, and a corporate job as mutually exclusive choices',
    body: 'Many MA graduates run a corporate or teaching job while preparing for NET or a State PSC exam in parallel. Picking exactly one door on day one and closing every other door is rarely necessary, and it removes your safety net.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Attempting UPSC on faith without checking your specific subject\'s actual overlap',
    body: 'History, political science, sociology, and public administration optionals genuinely overlap with the syllabus. Some other MA subjects overlap far less. Confirm the real syllabus match before betting years of prep on an assumption.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Assuming a PhD automatically leads to a permanent teaching post',
    body: 'A completed PhD funded by JRF, ICSSR, or another fellowship is a real credential, but permanent assistant professor vacancies open on their own schedule, not on your graduation date. Plan for a genuine wait after you finish, not an automatic landing.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Assuming the MA subject alone is a corporate skill signal',
    body: 'A masters in sociology, English, or history does not automatically read as "hire this person" to a private-sector recruiter the way a commerce degree with Excel or an engineering degree with a GitHub profile does. A certification, tool skill, or portfolio has to sit visibly on top of the subject.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Letting family or peer pressure choose the door instead of your actual runway',
    body: 'A relative\'s government job or a friend\'s PhD is one data point, not a plan for your specific finances, timeline, and risk tolerance. Run the 4-Fit Protocol above honestly before copying someone else\'s route.',
  },
];

const testSteps = [
  'Sit one real past NET or UPSC paper under exam conditions and score it honestly, instead of estimating your readiness from how the syllabus reads on paper.',
  'Ask one person already inside your target door — a JRF scholar, a PGT teacher, a State PSC officer, or a corporate hire in your field — what an ordinary week actually looks like, not the highlight-reel version.',
  'Apply for one real opening in the corporate or teaching lane even while academia or civil services stays your first choice, so you have live market feedback instead of a theory.',
  'Give the test as long as it genuinely takes. Some people know within a few weeks whether a door fits; others need a couple of months of real exposure and one honest conversation before the picture is clear.',
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What are the real career options after an MA in India?',
    a: 'The four main functioning doors are academia and research through UGC NET, JRF, and a PhD; civil services through UPSC or State PSC; teaching, either school-level (PGT/TGT) or college-level through NET; and corporate, policy, or industry roles once a multiplier skill sits on top of the MA. Most MA graduates realistically test more than one door before settling.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a PhD worth it after an MA in India?',
    a: 'It depends on funding and goal. A PhD funded through UGC NET JRF (roughly Rs 37,000-42,000 a month) or an ICSSR doctoral fellowship (roughly Rs 20,000 a month) is a genuine, low-debt research career path, typically 3-6 years long. It is worth it if you want to stay in the subject and can accept that a permanent teaching post is not automatic even after finishing. An unfunded PhD, taken mainly to delay a career decision, is a much weaker bet.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I get a government job just with an MA, without NET or UPSC?',
    a: 'Yes, in some lanes. SSC CGL, several State PSC posts, bank PO and specialist officer exams, and various PSU recruitment drives accept any postgraduate degree without requiring NET or a UPSC-specific optional. These are usually faster to a paycheck than the NET-to-teaching or UPSC-to-IAS routes, though the seniority ceiling differs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the salary after an MA in India in a corporate job?',
    a: 'Entry-level corporate, HR, content, or research-associate roles for MA graduates typically start around Rs 3-6 LPA (roughly Rs 25,000-50,000 a month), lower than a cleared government exam outcome, but starting months or years sooner. Pay rises meaningfully once a specific skill (data literacy, HR certification, content strategy, policy research methods) sits visibly on top of the subject.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I do NET or attempt UPSC directly after my MA?',
    a: 'NET is generally the lower-risk first move: it is subject-specific to your MA, funds a JRF-backed PhD if you clear the higher cutoff, and opens teaching eligibility either way. UPSC is a separate, much broader exam with an overall selection ratio near 0.1% in the 2025 cycle. Many MA graduates attempt NET first, then decide whether to also commit years to UPSC preparation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is teaching a good backup option after an MA?',
    a: 'Teaching is a real, structured career path, not a consolation prize, but only if you plan for it deliberately. School PGT/TGT posts through state boards or CTET-linked hiring pay sooner than the NET-to-college route; government pay (roughly Rs 30,000-55,000 a month) is meaningfully higher than most private-school pay for the same role, so it is worth targeting government hiring cycles specifically rather than defaulting to the nearest private school offer.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Career options after MA in India narrow down to four real doors: academia and research, civil services, teaching, and corporate or industry roles.</strong>
    Each one has its own entry exam or hiring route, its own pay ladder, and its own honest timeline to a first paycheck — and the right choice depends far less on which MA subject you hold than on your runway, your risk tolerance, and how soon you actually need income.
  </p>

  <p>
    Whichever door you pick, the real driver of long-term pay is the same across all four: a
    <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> layered on top of the MA — research output, exam
    subject-mastery, classroom or communication proof, or a market-facing certification — is what
    actually unlocks higher income inside that door, not the degree alone.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Academia (NET, JRF, PhD) and civil services (UPSC, State PSC) both ask for a long, low-income runway before any payoff, and neither guarantees a final outcome at the end.</li>
      <li>Teaching and corporate roles start paying sooner, with a lower ceiling upfront that rises fast once a real skill layer sits on top of the MA.</li>
      <li>These four doors are not mutually exclusive. Many MA graduates run a corporate or teaching income while preparing for NET or a State PSC exam in parallel.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose the door your actual runway and risk tolerance can support right now, and let the others stay open as a parallel plan, instead of betting everything on one exam result.</strong></li>
      <li>Do not stop at “get a job after MA.” Add AI research tools, communication, teaching or client skill, domain proof, and a route to consulting, training, content, research services, or another independent outcome where the subject supports it.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you want the wider map of what an arts, humanities, or social science background can lead to before narrowing to postgraduate doors specifically, start with <a href="/blog/career-options/">Career Options</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If choosing between these doors feels genuinely hard given your finances, timeline, or family pressure, <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">structured career guidance built around exactly this kind of decision</a> can help you map your specific situation instead of guessing.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="question">The real question after an MA</h2>

  <p>
    "What do I do with an MA" is not really about the subject anymore. By the time the degree is done, the subject has already given you what it is going to give you: a research base, a way of reading and arguing, maybe a specific syllabus overlap with an exam.
  </p>

  <p>
    The actual decision left standing is which of four structurally different doors to walk through, and each one asks a different question of your life, not just your transcript: how long can you go without stable income, how much risk of a zero outcome can you absorb, and how soon do you genuinely need to start earning.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">The usual bad advice</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>Just do NET, it keeps every door open.</li>
      <li>A PhD is always a safe, respectable choice.</li>
      <li>Teaching is the fallback if nothing else works.</li>
      <li>Government job or bust, private jobs are a downgrade.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      None of that tells you the actual JRF stipend, the real UPSC selection ratio, or how soon a corporate role can start paying while an exam attempt runs in parallel.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="doors">The 4 doors, compared</h2>

  <p>
    Here is the full map before the detail. Compare by runway and risk first, subject and pay second.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Door</th>
          <th>Best for</th>
          <th>Reality check</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {doorTable.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Door"><strong>{item.door}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Best for">{item.bestFor}</td>
            <td data-label="Reality check">{item.reality}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="academia">Academia and research: NET, JRF, and PhD</h2>

  <p>
    The door that keeps you working directly inside your MA subject, for people genuinely comfortable trading years of modest, funded income for a shot at a research or teaching career.
  </p>

  <div class="work-style-grid">
    {academiaCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="work-style-card">
        <div class="work-style-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="work-style-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      If UGC NET JRF does not come through, the ICSSR Doctoral Fellowship is a separate, real funding route for social science and humanities PhDs, requiring at least 55% in postgraduation and generally a NET qualification, paying roughly Rs 20,000 a month plus an annual contingency grant, for a strictly two-year term.
    </p>
    <p>
      Treat it as a second real option, not a rumour, but verify current eligibility and the application cycle on the official <a href="https://icssr.org/doctoral-fellowship" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ICSSR Doctoral Fellowship page</a> before planning around a specific figure.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Verify current stipend figures, JRF cutoffs, and vacancy notifications on the <a href="https://ugcnet.nta.nic.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official UGC NET portal</a> before planning a PhD funding route around a specific number.
  </p>

  <h2 id="civil-services">Civil services: UPSC and State PSC</h2>

  <p>
    The widest generalist government career available to any MA graduate, and the door most families default to recommending, often without checking the actual odds.
  </p>

  <div class="work-style-grid">
    {civilServicesCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="work-style-card">
        <div class="work-style-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="work-style-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Metric (CSE 2025 cycle)</th>
          <th>Figure</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Metric"><strong>Vacancies notified</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Figure">1,087 posts</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Metric"><strong>Candidates who appeared</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Figure">Roughly 5.77 lakh</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Metric"><strong>Candidates finally selected</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Figure">958</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Metric"><strong>Overall selection ratio</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Figure">Roughly 0.1%</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Verify current vacancy numbers, age limits, and attempt rules on the <a href="https://upsc.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official UPSC website</a> before planning attempts around them. If UPSC prep does not work out, <a href="/blog/career-options/upsc-alternative-careers-for-humanities-students-india/">UPSC alternative careers for humanities students</a> maps the pivot options in more depth.
  </p>

  <h2 id="teaching">Teaching: school PGT/TGT vs college</h2>

  <p>
    The door that starts paying soonest among the exam-gated options, and one that rewards deliberate targeting of government hiring cycles over settling for the nearest private-school offer.
  </p>

  <div class="work-style-grid">
    {teachingCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="work-style-card">
        <div class="work-style-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="work-style-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    If teaching genuinely interests you beyond it being a fallback, <a href="/blog/career-options/careers-for-people-who-love-to-teach-india/">careers for people who love to teach</a> covers the full teaching-adjacent map, including coaching and edtech.
  </p>

  <h2 id="corporate">Corporate, policy, and industry roles</h2>

  <p>
    The door with the least subject-specific gatekeeping and the fastest realistic path to a first paycheck, at the cost of the MA subject alone rarely being enough on its own.
  </p>

  <div class="work-style-grid">
    {corporateCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="work-style-card">
        <div class="work-style-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="work-style-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      No recruiter automatically reads "MA in sociology" or "MA in English" as "hire this person." A completed research project, a data or communication certification, or a portfolio of written work is what actually turns the degree into a signal a private-sector employer can act on.
    </p>
    <p>
      That single addition is usually the real difference between a corporate search that drags for eight months and one that closes in eight weeks. Building that
      <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> deliberately, rather than hoping the MA alone signals enough, is what actually unlocks high income opportunities in the corporate door — the same principle that decides pay ceilings in academia, civil services, and teaching too.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    If communication is your strongest transferable asset from the MA, <a href="/blog/career-options/career-options-for-people-good-at-communication-india/">career options for people good at communication</a> goes deeper into that specific direction.
  </p>

  <h2 id="other-govt">SSC, banking, and PSU exams beyond UPSC</h2>

  <p>
    UPSC and State PSC are not the only government route open to an MA graduate, and treating them as
    the only "real" government job is a common, avoidable mistake. Several other exam-gated doors
    accept any postgraduate degree, without requiring a UPSC-specific optional or years of General
    Studies prep.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>SSC CGL:</strong> opens central government roles across ministries and departments (Assistant, Inspector, and similar grades) to any postgraduate, with a shorter, more predictable prep cycle than UPSC CSE.</li>
    <li><strong>Bank PO and specialist officer exams (IBPS PO, SBI PO, RBI Grade B):</strong> accept any postgraduate degree, pay a structured, rising salary from day one, and generally have better odds than UPSC CSE, though still competitive.</li>
    <li><strong>PSU recruitment through non-technical hiring tracks:</strong> several public-sector undertakings run their own postgraduate-eligible drives for roles such as HR, corporate communications, and administration.</li>
    <li><strong>State-level Group B/C exams:</strong> often overlooked next to UPSC and the flagship State PSC exam, but genuinely faster to a paycheck and open to any MA subject.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="note-line">
    These routes are worth treating as genuine parallel options, not just a consolation prize if UPSC does not work out — several pay comparably to an early State PSC posting, with a shorter, less brutal odds curve.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost-timeline">The real cost and timeline map</h2>

  <p>
    The MA is rarely the finish line for any of these four doors. Run this math honestly before you commit a year of prep or fees to one route, not after it is already spent.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Stage</th>
          <th>What it actually costs, in time or money</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {costTimelineRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Stage"><strong>{row.item}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What it costs">{row.range}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
